## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to guarantee reliable cross-system delivery without distributed transactions, leveraging the Outbox pattern, idempotent consumption, and robust validation under partial failures.

## Key Concepts

- Outbox pattern
- Exactly-once delivery
- Idempotent consumers
- Durable Kafka keys
- Redis deduplication and caching

## Code Example

```sql
-- PostgreSQL tables
CREATE TABLE users (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  email TEXT,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE outbox (
  id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  aggregate_id UUID,
  event_type TEXT,
  payload JSONB,
  processed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(),
  last_attempt TIMESTAMPTZ
);
```

```sql
-- Atomic signup write
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO users (id, email) VALUES (?, ?);
INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_id, event_type, payload) VALUES (?, 'UserSignedUp', to_jsonb(?));
COMMIT;
```

